<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramiro I of Aragon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1007–<a href="/years/1063">1063</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimenez-dynasty">Jiménez dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramiro I was the first king of Aragon, ruling from 1035 to 1063. An illegitimate son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III of Navarre</a>, he inherited the county of Aragon on his father's death and governed it as an independent realm, adding Sobrarbe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a> around 1045 after the death of his half-brother Gonzalo. He was killed in 1063 while besieging Graus, a stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramiro-i-of-aragon.jpg" alt="Ramiro I of Aragon" width="256" height="582" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramirocely.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ramiro was born before 1007, the illegitimate son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III of Pamplona</a> and his mistress Sancha of Aybar. A story holds that his father's wife Muniadona adopted him because he alone of Sancho's children came to her aid, but no record of these events survives and the story is probably apocryphal. He appears as a witness of royal charters from 1011 and was given numerous properties in the county of Aragon.</p><p>When Sancho's realm was divided on his death in 1035, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and the Basque country went to the eldest half-brother García, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> to Ferdinand, and Sobrarbe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a> to Gonzalo. Ramiro received the county of Aragon with the title of baiulus, or steward, to hold under García.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ramiro's status was vague. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, neighbours, the church and even his sons called him king, yet he styled himself only Ramiro, son of King Sancho, and in his two wills spoke of his lands as held in stewardship, first from García and then from God. Charters from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> call him regulus, not rex, and quasi pro rege, acting as if king. Because of his growing independence and the small size of his Pyrenean holdings he is sometimes called a petty king and Aragon a pocket kingdom.</p><p>He sought to enlarge his lands at the expense of both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> and García. After his father's death he supported the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a> in an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>; he was defeated at the Battle of Tafalla but gained territory, including Sangüesa, and established a state of semi-autonomy. With García's apparent approval he annexed Sobrarbe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a>, creating a pseudo-independent state with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaca">Jaca</a>. He then began to advance towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>. The first charter for the royal town of Jaca is attributed to him; it gave protection even to non-residents and set an example for urban rights late into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ramiro died in 1063 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-graus">Battle of Graus</a> while trying to take the city, and was buried at the monastery of San Juan de la Peña. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-ramirez">Sancho Ramírez</a> succeeded him as king of Aragon. By his first marriage, in 1036 to Gisberga, daughter of Bernard Roger of Bigorre, he had five children, among them García, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaca">Jaca</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ramiro I of Aragon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Sancho III the Great of Navarre died in 1035, his illegitimate son <b>Ramiro</b> I received Aragon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Aragon was ruled from Pamplona until Sancho III died in 1035 and left it to his son <b>Ramiro I</b>, who governed it as a separate realm, the beginning of the Kingdom of Aragon, which later grew into the Crown of Aragon.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-aragon">County of Aragon</a></li><li><blockquote>After Gonzalo's death around 1045 it went to <b>Ramiro I</b>, joining the Kingdom of Aragon.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-ribagorza">County of Ribagorza</a></li><li><blockquote>On his death in 1035 Sobrarbe and Ribagorza went to his son Gonzalo, and after Gonzalo's murder around 1045 they passed to <b>Ramiro I of Aragon</b>, becoming part of the Kingdom of Aragon.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-sobrarbe">County of Sobrarbe</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Aibar/Oibar, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Graus, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.59,-1.36&amp;year=1043" aria-label="Aibar/Oibar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4517 650 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ramiro I of Aragon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4967 920h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4967 920h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.59,-1.36&amp;year=1043">What was here in 1043? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
